[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*For 8 000 trail segments you can start with a plain **GeoJSON source** – it will almost certainly stay under the “sweet-spot” (< 2 MB, < 5 000 features) if you simplify the line geometries and trim coordinate precision.  
Add a **vector-tile basemap** (or a hosted style URL that already includes terrain) and, if you want true 3-D terrain, a **raster-dem** source for elevation.  

Only switch to a **vector-tile source for the trails** if the GeoJSON file grows beyond ~5 MB (or you notice > 2 s parse lag on typical devices). In that case generate tiles with *tippecanoe* or *Planetiler* and add them as a `vector` source.*

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the code you can drop into a MapLibre GL JS app.

---

## 1. Estimate the GeoJSON size

| # of features | Typical line-segment size (≈ 200 bytes) | Approx. file size |
|---------------|----------------------------------------|-------------------|
| 8 000         | 200 B (after simplifying)             | **≈ 1.6 MB**      |

*If you keep the geometry simple (no unnecessary intermediate vertices) and round coordinates to 5-6 decimal places, you’ll stay comfortably under the 2 MB “sweet-spot”.*  

If you later add elevation points, more vertices, or richer properties, re-measure the file size. Anything above **5 MB** (or > 50 000 features) is the “lag zone” where you should start testing vector tiles.

---

## 2. Choose the basemap

A terrain-aware basemap is most easily obtained from a **vector-tile style URL** that already bundles:

* land-cover, water, roads, place-labels (vector)
* a `raster-dem` source for hill-shading / 3-D terrain (optional)

Examples of ready-made style URLs:

| Provider | Style URL (example) | Terrain? |
|----------|---------------------|----------|
| MapTiler | `https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | Yes (raster-dem) |
| OpenFreeMap | `https://free.tilehosting.com/styles/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | No (add your own DEM) |
| OSM-France (PMTiles) | `https://.../osm-france.pmtiles` (use `maplibre-gl-pmtiles` plugin) | No |

Pick the one that matches the visual style you want. If the style **does not** include a DEM, you can add a separate `raster-dem` source (see § 4).

---

## 3. Add your trails as a **GeoJSON source**

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map with the basemap style URL
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  center: [-122.45, 37.75], // whatever centre you need
  zoom: 10
});

// 2️⃣ When the style is loaded, add the trail source + layer
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // ---- GEOJSON source -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('trails', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: '/data/trails.geojson',   // or an inline object
    // optional: enable clustering if you later have many points
    // cluster: true,
    // clusterRadius: 50
  });

  // ---- LINE layer ----------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'trails-line',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'trails',
    layout: {
      // Insert *before* the first symbol layer so your lines stay under labels
      // (see maplibre-source-wiring for a helper function)
      'line-cap': 'round',
      'line-join': 'round'
    },
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ff7f00',
      'line-width': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['zoom'],
        10, 2,
        14, 4,
        18, 8
      ]
    }
  });

  // ---- POPUP on click ------------------------------------------------
  map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
    const props = e.features[0].properties;
    const name = props.name || 'Unnamed trail';
    const dist = props.distance_km ? `${props.distance_km} km` : '';
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>${dist}`)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change cursor on hover (optional)
  map.on('mouseenter', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
});
```

### Why this works

* **All 8 000 line features are fetched once** (the whole GeoJSON file).  
* The browser parses the file (≈ 1 – 2 s on a typical desktop, < 1 s on a modern phone).  
* MapLibre renders the lines client-side, so you can query any property (`name`, `distance_km`) without a server round-trip.  
* The basemap stays completely separate – it is still requested as vector tiles, so you get crisp labels, terrain shading, and low bandwidth usage for the background.

---

## 4. Adding true 3-D terrain (optional)

If the basemap style you chose **does not** already include a DEM, add one yourself:

```javascript
// Add a raster-dem source (e.g. MapTiler DEM tiles)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY',
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: maxzoom: 14,
  // optional: attribution: '© MapTiler'
});

// Enable the terrain rendering
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

*The `setTerrain` call tells MapLibre to use the DEM for hill-shading and 3-D extrusion. It works together with any vector basemap.*

---

## 5. When to **upgrade to vector tiles** for the trails

Switch only if one of these conditions appears:

| Condition | What to do |
|-----------|------------|
| **File size > 5 MB** after you’ve simplified the geometry | Generate a vector-tile set (see below) and add it as a `vector` source. |
| **Parsing delay > 2 s on target devices** (mobile, low-end tablets) | Same as above – tile the data. |
| **You need different levels of detail** (e.g., show only the trail centreline at low zoom, and the full geometry at high zoom) | Vector tiles let you encode simplified geometries at low zoom levels automatically. |
| **Future-proofing** – you anticipate the dataset growing beyond 10 000 features | Start with tiles now; the workflow is the same later. |

### Quick tip-to-tile workflow (if you need it)

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install tippecanoe (Linux/macOS)
brew install tippecanoe   # macOS
# or: sudo apt-get install tippecanoe

# 2️⃣ Generate an MBTiles file (zoom 0-14, 5 m tolerance)
tippecanoe \
  -o trails.mbtiles \
  -l trails \
  -z14 -Z0 \
  --drop-densest-as-needed \
  --simplify-only-low-zoom \
  --coalesce-densest-as-needed \
  --force \
  trails.geojson

# 3️⃣ Serve the MBTiles (e.g. with pmtiles)
#    npm i -g @maplibre/pmtiles
pmtiles serve trails.mbtiles --port 8080
```

Then in MapLibre:

```javascript
map.addSource('trails', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'pmtiles://http://localhost:8080/trails.pmtiles' // or a TileJSON URL
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  'source-layer': 'trails',   // name you gave with -l
  paint: { 'line-color': '#ff7f00', 'line-width': 3 }
});
```

*Tip:* Keep the **GeoJSON** version around for quick development (you can hot-reload it) and switch to the tiled version for production builds.

---

## 6. Layer ordering – keep labels readable

When you add your own line layer **after** the basemap style loads, it will be placed on top of everything (including the basemap’s label symbols). That’s usually fine for lines, but if you add **polygon fills** you’ll want them *under* the label symbols. The easiest pattern:

```javascript
// Find the first symbol layer in the style
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert your layer just before that layer
map.addLayer(trailLayer, firstSymbolId);
```

(See the `maplibre-source-wiring` skill for a reusable helper.)

---

## 7. Summary checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **GeoJSON** for 8 000 trail segments (≈ 1-2 MB) – ✅ |
| **Simplify** geometry (remove unnecessary vertices, round coordinates to 5-6 decimals) – ✅ |
| **Vector-tile basemap** (style URL) – ✅ |
| **Optional raster-dem** for 3-D terrain – ✅ |
| **Layer order** – insert before first symbol layer if you add fills – ✅ |
| **Performance test** – open the map on a typical mobile device; if load > 2 s, consider tiling – ✅ |
| **Future growth** – keep tippecanoe/Planetiler scripts handy – ✅ |

If after testing you see a noticeable lag, run the tiny tippecanoe command above and replace the GeoJSON source with a `vector` source. Otherwise, stay with GeoJSON – it’s the simplest, most flexible solution for a dataset of this size and gives you instant client-side interactivity.

Below is a step-by-step recipe for turning a single scanned JPG (or PNG) into a **georeferenced image overlay** that you can turn on/off or fade with an opacity slider in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1. Choose the right source type – `image`

| What you have | What you need | MapLibre source type |
|---------------|---------------|----------------------|
| One raster file (JPG/PNG) that you already know the four corner (Lon/Lat) coordinates | Show it at that exact spot, no tiling required | **`image`** (single-image source) |

`image` sources are the simplest way to display a scanned map, historic map, scanned chart, etc. They are fetched once (or cached) and drawn as a raster layer. No tile server is needed.

> **When to consider a tiled raster source instead?**  
> If the JPG is > 5 MB, or you need to support many simultaneous users on a low-bandwidth network, you may want to pre-tile it into XYZ or PMTiles and use a `raster` source. The workflow below works for the single-image case; the “tiled raster” alternative is mentioned at the end.

---

## 2. Prepare the corner coordinates

MapLibre expects the corners in **counter-clockwise order** starting with the **top-left** (north-west) corner:

```text
[
  [lonNW, latNW],   // top-left
  [lonNE, latNE],   // top-right
  [lonSE, latSE],   // bottom-right
  [lonSW, latSW]    // bottom-left
]
```

If you only have the four corners in a different order, just reorder them.

*Tip:* Write them as a JavaScript array literal so you can copy-paste into the source definition.

---

## 3. Add the `image` source to the map

```javascript
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('historic-map', {
  type: 'image',
  url: 'https://example.com/maps/1920s-city.jpg',   // <-- your JPG URL
  coordinates: [
    [-122.523, 37.833],   // top-left (NW)
    [-122.354, 37.833],   // top-right (NE)
    [-122.354, 37.704],   // bottom-right (SE)
    [-122.523, 37.704]    // bottom-left (SW)
  ]
});
```

**CORS note** – The image must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can read it. If you control the server, add that header; otherwise host the file on a CORS-friendly CDN (e.g. GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare R2, S3 with public-read CORS).

---

## 4. Create a raster layer that draws the image

```javascript
map.addLayer({
  id: 'historic-map-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'historic-map',
  paint: {
    // Start fully opaque; you’ll change this later with UI
    'raster-opacity': 1.0
  }
});
```

### Layer ordering

If you want the historic map **under** the basemap labels (so labels stay readable), insert the layer *before* the first symbol layer:

```javascript
// Find the first symbol layer in the style (usually labels)
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Insert the historic map just before it
map.addLayer({
  id: 'historic-map-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'historic-map',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
}, firstSymbolId);
```

If you want the historic map on top (e.g., a semi-transparent overlay), just add it without the `beforeId` argument.

---

## 5. UI for toggling / fading opacity

### Simple on/off checkbox

```html
<label>
  <input type="checkbox" id="toggle-historic" checked>
  Show 1920s map
</label>
```

```javascript
document.getElementById('toggle-historic').addEventListener('change', e => {
  const visible = e.target.checked;
  map.setLayoutProperty(
    'historic-map-layer',
    'visibility',
    visible ? 'visible' : 'none'
  );
});
```

### Slider for smooth opacity control

```html
<label>
  Opacity:
  <input type="range" id="opacity-slider" min="0" max="100" value="100">
</label>
```

```javascript
document.getElementById('opacity-slider').addEventListener('input', e => {
  const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100;   // 0-1
  map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
});
```

Both controls can coexist – the checkbox toggles visibility, the slider adjusts the alpha of the visible layer.

---

## 6. (Optional) Turn the single image into raster tiles

If the JPG is large (e.g., > 5 MB) or you expect many concurrent users, you can pre-tile it:

1. **Tile the image** – tools like **`gdal2tiles.py`**, **`maptiler`**, or **`tilemaker`** can split a georeferenced image into XYZ tiles.  
   *You’ll need a GeoTIFF with the same corner coordinates; GDAL can convert the JPG → GeoTIFF and embed the geotransform.*

2. **Host the tiles** – static file host (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages) or a lightweight tile server (tileserver-gl, TileServer-PHP).

3. **Use a `raster` source** instead of `image`:

   ```javascript
   map.addSource('historic-tiles', {
     type: 'raster',
     tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
     tileSize: 256,
     // optional: bounds: [west, south, east, north] to limit requests
   });
   ```

   The rest of the layer code (opacity, UI) stays the same.

> **Why bother?**  
> * Faster initial load (only the tiles you need).  
> * Better caching – browsers cache individual tiles, not a huge single image.  
> * Works even on low-memory devices (mobile browsers).

For a one-off demo or a small city map, the `image` source is usually sufficient.

---

## 7. Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Historic Map Overlay</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control { position:absolute; top:10px; left:10px; background:#fff; padding:8px; border-radius:4px; font-family:sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control">
  <label><input type="checkbox" id="toggle-historic" checked> Show historic map</label><br>
  Opacity: <input type="range" id="opacity-slider" min="0" max="100" value="100">
</div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any basemap style you like
    center: [-122.44, 37.77],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the image source
    map.addSource('historic-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://example.com/1920s-city.jpg',
      coordinates: [
        [-122.523, 37.833], // NW
        [-122.354, 37.833], // NE
        [-122.354, 37.704], // SE
        [-122.523, 37.704]  // SW
      ]
    });

    // 2️⃣ Insert the raster layer just under the first symbol layer (labels)
    const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historic-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historic-map',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1.0 }
    }, firstSymbol);

    // 3️⃣ UI wiring
    document.getElementById('toggle-historic').addEventListener('change', e => {
      map.setLayoutProperty('historic-map-layer', 'visibility',
        e.target.checked ? 'visible' : 'none');
    });

    document.getElementById('opacity-slider').addEventListener('input', e => {
      const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100;
      map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Copy-paste, replace the `url` and `coordinates` with your own values, and you’re done.

---

## 8. Checklist & Gotchas

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **CORS headers** on the JPG | Without them the browser blocks the image and you’ll see a console error `Cross origin image load denied` |
| **Correct corner order** (NW, NE, SE, SW) | Wrong order skews, flips, or places the image in the wrong quadrant |
| **Layer order** – insert before label symbols if you want labels on top | Otherwise the historic raster will cover all text |
| **Opacity range** – keep it between 0 and 1 (`raster-opacity`) | Values outside that range are clamped but may cause warnings |
| **Tile size** – default 256 px works for most images; you can set `tileSize: 512` if you want higher-resolution raster tiles (only relevant for tiled raster sources) |
| **Performance** – if the JPG is > 5 MB, consider tiling (see § 6) | Large single images can cause a noticeable pause on slow connections |
| **Attribution** – add any required historic map credit in your UI or style’s `metadata` | Legal compliance; MapLibre does not enforce it automatically |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Add an `image` source** with the four corner coordinates.  
2. **Add a `raster` layer** that uses that source.  
3. **Control visibility** (`visibility` layout property) and **opacity** (`raster-opacity` paint property) from UI elements (checkbox, range slider).  
4. Ensure **CORS** is enabled on the image host and that the layer is ordered correctly relative to other map layers.

That’s the “right way” to overlay a scanned historic map on a MapLibre GL JS map and let users turn its opacity up or down. Happy mapping!

**Use a **`vector`** tile source for the basemap.**  

Vector tiles contain the raw geometry and feature attributes only once; the visual appearance (colors, line-weights, label styles, etc.) is defined in the **style** that you give to MapLibre. Because the style is separate from the tile data, you can swap between a light-theme style and a dark-theme style at runtime without ever touching the tile URLs.

### Why vector tiles are the right choice

| Tile type | How the map looks | What changes when you switch themes |
|-----------|-------------------|--------------------------------------|
| **vector** | Geometry + attributes are fetched once. The style (JSON) decides colors, fonts, opacity, etc. | You only replace or modify the *style* (or toggle layer paint/layout properties). No extra network requests for a second tile set. |
| **raster** | Pre-rendered PNG/JPEG images already contain the colors for a single theme. | To get a different theme you need a completely separate raster tile set (light vs. dark) → double the storage, bandwidth, and CORS configuration. |
| **geojson** | Whole dataset is downloaded as a single file. | You could restyle it client-side, but large GeoJSONs quickly hit the performance limits (≈ 2 MB / 5 k features). Not suitable for a full-world basemap. |

So the **single source** you host is a vector tile service (e.g. an MBTiles/PMTiles file, a TileJSON endpoint, or a hosted provider like MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.). The two themes are expressed as two separate **style JSONs** (or as one style that you programmatically toggle paint/layout values).

### Typical implementation pattern

```js
// 1️⃣ Load the vector tile source once (shared by both themes)
const vectorSource = {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'   // TileJSON or PMTiles URL
};

// 2️⃣ Define two style objects (light & dark). They reference the same source name.
const lightStyle = {
  version: 8,
  sources: { basemap: vectorSource },
  layers: [
    // ... layers that use light colors, e.g. water: '#a0c8f0'
    // make sure to insert your own data layers *before* the first symbol layer
  ],
  glyphs: 'https://my.fonts.com/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf',
  sprite: 'https://my.sprites.com/sprite'
};

const darkStyle = {
  version: 8,
  sources: { basemap: vectorSource },
  layers: [
    // ... same layer structure, but with dark colors, e.g. water: '#0b3d91'
  ],
  glyphs: 'https://my.fonts.com/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf',
  sprite: 'https://my.sprites.com/sprite'
};

// 3️⃣ Initialise the map with one of the styles
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: lightStyle,          // start with light theme
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 4️⃣ Switch themes at runtime (e.g. on a button click)
document.getElementById('themeToggle').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const newStyle = map.getStyle().metadata?.theme === 'dark' ? lightStyle : darkStyle;
  // optional: store current theme in metadata so we can read it later
  newStyle.metadata = { theme: newStyle === darkStyle ? 'dark' : 'light' };
  map.setStyle(newStyle);
});
```

**Key points**

* The **source definition (`type: 'vector'`) is identical** in both style objects, so the browser only ever requests the same tile URLs.
* Switching the style with `map.setStyle()` (or by updating paint/layout properties via `setPaintProperty` / `setLayoutProperty`) is instantaneous after the new style JSON is loaded; the vector tiles are already cached.
* If you want to keep a single style file and just toggle colors, you can also keep one style and, on the toggle, call `map.setPaintProperty()` for each layer (or use a CSS-like “theme” object you apply programmatically). This avoids a second network request for the style JSON itself.

### When you *might* need two raster sets

Only if you are forced to use **raster** tiles (e.g., you have only satellite imagery that cannot be vectorized) would you need separate light/dark raster layers. In that case you would host two raster tile URLs and toggle the source ID in the style, but you would pay double the storage and bandwidth. For a typical basemap that includes land, water, roads, and labels, vector tiles are the standard and recommended approach.

### TL;DR

* **Choose `vector` tiles** for the basemap.  
* Keep a **single vector source** and create **two style JSONs** (light & dark) that reference that source.  
* Switch styles at runtime with `map.setStyle()` (or by updating paint/layout properties).  

That gives you theme switching with **no extra tile hosting**, minimal bandwidth, and full client-side control over the look of the map.

**Short answer:**  
Yes. Leaflet can show a MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap, but it can’t read the vector tiles itself. You have to embed a tiny MapLibre GL JS map inside your Leaflet map using the **`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin (or migrate the whole app to MapLibre GL JS).  

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus the “why” and a few gotchas.

---

## 1. Why you need the plugin

| Library | Can read vector tiles? | What it renders |
|---------|-----------------------|-----------------|
| **Leaflet** | **No** – only raster tiles (PNG/JPEG/WebP) or GeoJSON | Raster imagery, markers, pop-ups |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | **Yes** – `vector` source (MVT/MLT) | Full style spec, feature-query, crisp text |
| **MapLibre-GL-Leaflet** | Bridges the two – creates a *Leaflet layer* that internally runs a MapLibre GL map | Vector-tile basemap + any Leaflet overlays you already have |

The plugin does **not** convert vector tiles to raster; it just draws the MapLibre GL canvas as a Leaflet overlay, so you keep all the benefits of vector tiles (style changes, interactivity, tiny bandwidth).

---

## 2. Install the required packages

If you use a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.):

```bash
npm i maplibre-gl @maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet
# optional – if you need a style editor locally
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-style-spec
```

If you prefer plain `<script>` tags, use the CDN versions (the plugin is published on unpkg):

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link   href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version note** – The plugin tracks MapLibre GL JS ≥ 4.0. If you are on an older MapLibre GL version, upgrade first.

---

## 3. Create the Leaflet map as usual

```js
// Basic Leaflet map (centered on London, zoom 10)
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [51.505, -0.09],
  zoom: 10,
  // optional: set maxZoom to match the vector tile source
  maxZoom: 18
});
```

You can still add any Leaflet layers you already use (markers, GeoJSON, heatmaps, etc.) – they will sit **above** the vector-tile basemap because the plugin adds the MapLibre canvas as a *single* Leaflet layer.

---

## 4. Add the MapLibre vector-tile basemap

```js
// 1️⃣ Choose a style URL that points to a MapLibre style JSON.
//    Public options: OpenStreetMap (OpenFreeMap), MapTiler, OSM Bright, etc.
//    Example: MapTiler's free “basic” style (requires an API key)
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY';

// 2️⃣ Create the MapLibre-GL-Leaflet layer
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // Pass the style URL (or an inline style object)
  style: styleUrl,

  // Optional: if you need to tweak the MapLibre map before it renders
  // you can provide a `mapOptions` object (same keys as new maplibregl.Map)
  // e.g. { hash: true, preserveDrawingBuffer: true }
  // mapOptions: { ... },

  // Optional: set the attribution that will be added to Leaflet's control
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

That’s it – the vector-tile basemap now appears under all your existing Leaflet layers.

### 4.1. Controlling layer order

Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added. If you need to insert the GL layer **between** other Leaflet layers (e.g., you want a raster overlay *under* the basemap), use the `addTo`/`addLayer` methods with a specific `pane`:

```js
// Create a custom pane with a higher z-index than the GL layer
leafletMap.createPane('rasterOverlay');
leafletMap.getPane('rasterOverlay').style.zIndex = 450; // GL layer defaults to 400

L.tileLayer('https://my.raster.server/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  pane: 'rasterOverlay',
  attribution: 'My raster source'
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

---

## 5. Adding your own data on top (GeoJSON, markers, etc.)

Because the basemap is now a Leaflet layer, you can keep using the familiar Leaflet APIs:

```js
// Example: a small GeoJSON overlay (well under the 2 MB / 5 k-feature sweet spot)
fetch('/data/parks.geojson')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(geojson => {
    L.geoJSON(geojson, {
      style: {color: '#2ecc71', weight: 2},
      onEachFeature: (f, layer) => {
        layer.bindPopup(`Park: ${f.properties.name}`);
      }
    }).addTo(leafletMap);
  });
```

If your own dataset grows beyond the GeoJSON thresholds (≈ 5 k features / 2 MB), switch it to its own **vector tile source** and add it as a second `L.maplibreGL` layer (or merge it into the same style JSON as a separate source). The plugin supports multiple GL layers; just give each a distinct `zIndex` via Leaflet panes.

---

## 6. Handling CORS & Attribution

* **CORS** – The vector-tile service must allow cross-origin requests from your domain (or you must serve the style via a proxy). Most public providers set `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. If you self-host, configure your web server (nginx, Apache, CloudFront, etc.) accordingly.

* **Attribution** – The MapLibre style JSON usually contains an `attribution` field. The plugin copies it into Leaflet’s attribution control, but you can also supply your own string (see the `attribution` option above). Make sure you respect the basemap provider’s licensing (e.g., “© OpenStreetMap contributors”).

---

## 7. When you *don’t* need the plugin

If you only need a **raster** basemap (satellite imagery, WMS, WMTS), you can stay purely in Leaflet:

```js
L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

But you lose the advantages of vector tiles (style flexibility, crisp labels, feature queries). The plugin is the lightweight way to keep those benefits while preserving the rest of your Leaflet codebase.

---

## 8. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre vector basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet"
        href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <style>#map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link   href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
      rel="stylesheet"/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Leaflet map
  const map = L.map('map', {center: [51.505, -0.09], zoom: 10});

  // 2️⃣ Vector-tile basemap via MapLibre GL Leaflet
  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY',
    attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Optional Leaflet overlay (marker)
  L.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)
    .bindPopup('A Leaflet marker on top of a vector basemap')
    .openPopup();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with a free MapTiler key (or use any other public style URL).

---

## 9. When you might prefer a full migration to MapLibre GL JS

* You need **per-feature interactivity** (click/hover) on the basemap itself (e.g., query road names).  
* You want to **dynamically switch styles** (dark / light mode) without re-creating Leaflet layers.  
* You plan to add **multiple vector tile sources** (e.g., basemap + your own tiles) and want fine-grained control over `source-layer` ordering.

In those cases, replace the Leaflet map entirely:

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_API_KEY',
  center: [ -0.09, 51.505 ],
  zoom: 10
});
```

Then add Leaflet-style interactions via MapLibre’s own API (`map.on('click', ...)`, `map.addLayer`, etc.). The migration guide linked in the skill (Leaflet migration guide) walks you through the exact steps.

---

## 10. TL;DR checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| 1 | Install `maplibre-gl` and `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`. |
| 2 | Create your normal Leaflet map (`L.map`). |
| 3 | Add a `L.maplibreGL({style: <style-url>})` layer to the map. |
| 4 | Keep all existing Leaflet overlays (markers, GeoJSON, heatmaps) – they automatically render on top. |
| 5 | Ensure the vector-tile service allows CORS and you display required attribution. |
| 6 | If your own data exceeds ~2 MB / 5 k features, serve it as a vector-tile source (add a second `L.maplibreGL` layer or merge into the same style). |
| 7 | For heavy interactivity or many vector sources, consider migrating the whole app to MapLibre GL JS. |

That’s everything you need to drop a MapLibre-style vector basemap into a Leaflet application while keeping the rest of your Leaflet code untouched. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step migration path** that takes you from a pure Leaflet app (raster tile layers + `L.geoJSON` overlays) to a single **MapLibre GL JS** map that:

1. **Keeps any existing raster tiles** (e.g. a base-map or WMS layer) as a `raster` source.  
2. **Turns your own data into vector tiles** (or, if it’s still tiny, leaves it as a `geojson` source).  
3. **Writes a style JSON** that mirrors the layer order you had in Leaflet.  
4. **Drops Leaflet completely** – you no longer need a second map instance.

Everything you need to map from Leaflet APIs to MapLibre APIs is listed, plus the most useful reference docs and tooling.

---

## 1️⃣ Decide the source type for each piece of data

| What you have now | Recommended MapLibre source | When you can stay with that source |
|-------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------------|
| **Raster tile layer** (`L.tileLayer('https://…/{z}/{x}/{y}.png')`) | `raster` source | Always – raster tiles are cheap to keep as-is. |
| **Small GeoJSON overlay** (≤ 2 MB / ≈ 5 k features) | `geojson` source (inline or URL) | Keep as GeoJSON – no tile server needed. |
| **Medium-large or growing GeoJSON** (5 k – 100 k features, > 5 MB) | `vector` source (tiles) | Generate vector tiles (Tippecanoe, Planetiler, or any PMTiles server). |
| **Very large / global data** (> 100 k features, > 50 MB) | `vector` source (tiles) | Must be tiled. |

> **Why this matters** – MapLibre downloads a whole GeoJSON file on every load. The thresholds in the *MapLibre Tile Sources* skill (sweet-spot < 2 MB, lag 5-20 MB, crash > 50 MB) are the rule-of-thumb you should follow.

---

## 2️⃣ Generate (or serve) your own vector tiles – if you need them

| Tool | What it does | Typical command (GeoJSON → MBTiles) |
|------|--------------|--------------------------------------|
| **Tippecanoe** (Felt) | Fast, command-line tile generator for static GeoJSON | `tippecanoe -o data.mbtiles -zg --drop-densest-as-needed data.geojson` |
| **Planetiler** (OnTheGoMap) | Java-based, OSM-style pipeline; can read Parquet, FlatGeobuf, etc. | `java -jar planetiler.jar --input data.parquet --output data.pmtiles` |
| **PMTiles server** (static file) | Serves vector tiles directly from a single `.pmtiles` file (no backend) | Drop `data.pmtiles` in a CDN or S3 bucket; use `maplibre-gl-js` `pmtiles` plugin to read it. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a raster basemap from a provider (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.) you can keep that URL and only tile *your* data. That avoids the heavy work of re-tiling the whole world.

---

## 3️⃣ Build a MapLibre style JSON

A style is just a JSON object that lists **sources** and **layers**. The easiest way to get a starter style is to copy the one you used in Leaflet (if you built it with a provider) and then add your own sources/layers.

### 3.1 Add the sources

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": [
        "https://my.raster.provider/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "attribution": "© Provider"
    },

    /* If your overlay is still small enough for GeoJSON */
    "my-geojson": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "https://my.server/overlays/my-data.geojson"
    },

    /* If you generated vector tiles */
    "my-vector": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://my.cdn/data.pmtiles"   // or a TileJSON URL
    }
  },

  "layers": [ /* … will be filled in next … */ ]
}
```

*If you use a `pmtiles://` URL you need to load the **pmtiles** plugin first (see the “PMTiles pattern” skill).*

### 3.2 Translate each Leaflet layer to a MapLibre layer

| Leaflet API | MapLibre equivalent | Typical `type` value | Example |
|-------------|--------------------|----------------------|---------|
| `L.tileLayer` | `raster` source + a `raster` layer | `raster` | `{ "id":"basemap", "type":"raster", "source":"basemap" }` |
| `L.geoJSON(..., {style: {...}})` | `geojson` source + a `fill` / `line` / `circle` layer | `fill`, `line`, `circle` | See code below |
| `L.geoJSON(..., {pointToLayer: (f, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng)})` | `geojson` source + a `circle` layer (or `symbol` with an icon) | `circle` | … |
| `L.geoJSON(..., {onEachFeature: …})` (click/hover) | `map.on('click', 'layer-id', e => …)` | — | … |

#### Example: a polygon overlay that used Leaflet’s `style` option

**Leaflet**

```js
L.geoJSON(polygonData, {
  style: {color: '#0080ff', weight: 2, fillOpacity: 0.3}
}).addTo(map);
```

**MapLibre style fragment**

```json
{
  "id": "my-polygons",
  "type": "fill",
  "source": "my-geojson",          // or "my-vector" if tiled
  "source-layer": "polygons",      // only needed for vector tiles
  "paint": {
    "fill-color": "#0080ff",
    "fill-opacity": 0.3,
    "fill-outline-color": "#0080ff"
  }
}
```

If you used a **vector tile source**, you must know the *source-layer* name that tippecanoe/Planetiler wrote (default is the filename without extension, or you can set `--layer=name`). You can inspect a tile with `mapbox-vector-tile` or the browser dev tools.

#### Example: point data that used Leaflet’s `circleMarker`

**Leaflet**

```js
L.geoJSON(pointData, {
  pointToLayer: (f, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, {radius: 6, fillColor: '#f00'})
}).addTo(map);
```

**MapLibre style fragment**

```json
{
  "id": "my-points",
  "type": "circle",
  "source": "my-geojson",
  "paint": {
    "circle-radius": 6,
    "circle-color": "#ff0000",
    "circle-stroke-width": 1,
    "circle-stroke-color": "#fff"
  }
}
```

#### Example: click interaction

```js
map.on('click', 'my-polygons', e => {
  const props = e.features[0].properties;
  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong>`)
    .addTo(map);
});
```

That replaces Leaflet’s `layer.on('click', …)`.

---

## 4️⃣ Assemble the final style JSON (order matters)

Layers are drawn **in the order they appear**. To keep your overlay *under* the basemap’s labels (the most common requirement), insert your custom layers **before** the first symbol layer of the basemap.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … as above … },

  "layers": [
    /* 1️⃣ Your custom layers first */
    { "id":"my-polygons", "type":"fill",   "source":"my-geojson", … },
    { "id":"my-points",   "type":"circle", "source":"my-geojson", … },

    /* 2️⃣ Then the basemap raster layer */
    { "id":"basemap", "type":"raster", "source":"basemap" },

    /* 3️⃣ Finally the basemap’s label layers (usually already in the provider style) */
    /* you can just copy them from the provider’s style JSON */
  ]
}
```

If you are using a **hosted vector basemap style URL** (e.g. `https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=…`), you can **extend** it instead of rewriting it from scratch:

```js
maplibregl.accessToken = 'YOUR_KEY';
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ add your own source(s)
  map.addSource('my-data', {type:'geojson', data:'/data/my-data.geojson'});
  // 2️⃣ add layers before the first symbol layer
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  const insertBefore = firstSymbol ? firstSymbol.id : undefined;

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-polygons',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: { 'fill-color': '#0080ff', 'fill-opacity': 0.4 }
  }, insertBefore);
});
```

That pattern is exactly what the **maplibre-source-wiring** skill documents.

---

## 5️⃣ Drop Leaflet – the final HTML/JS skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre only</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<!-- If you use PMTiles -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/pmtiles@2.5.0/dist/pmtiles.js"></script>

<script>
  // Register PMTiles protocol (optional)
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "sources": {
        "basemap": {
          "type": "raster",
          "tiles": ["https://my.raster.provider/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          "tileSize": 256
        },
        "my-data": {
          "type": "geojson",
          "data": "https://my.server/overlays/my-data.geojson"
        }
        /* or "my-data": { "type":"vector", "url":"pmtiles://https://my.cdn/data.pmtiles" } */
      },
      "layers": [
        { "id":"my-polygons", "type":"fill", "source":"my-data",
          "paint":{"fill-color":"#0080ff","fill-opacity":0.3} },
        { "id":"basemap", "type":"raster", "source":"basemap" }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Example click handler
  map.on('click', 'my-polygons', e => {
    const props = e.features[0].properties;
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong>`)
      .addTo(map);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

That file replaces the whole Leaflet `<script>` block. No `L.*` objects remain.

---

## 6️⃣
